        "evidence": "Unpatched run output: \"served file: /tmp/pov_ext_base_secret/secret.txt\", \"served content: TOP_SECRET_OUTSIDE_STATIC_ROOT\", \"escapes configured folder: true\", exit 0. After temporarily applying a boundary-aware fix (!p.equals(base) && !p.startsWith(base + \"/\")) and rebuilding, the same command printed \"BLOCKED: traversal detected -> external\" and exited 1. A control payload \"/..\\..\\etc\\passwd\" is correctly blocked even on the unpatched code, confirming the PoV isolates the prefix-boundary flaw. Product file was reverted after testing; only .security-pipeline/ remains untracked.",
        "expected_vulnerable_behavior": "The handler resolves the attack path \"/..\\pov_ext_base_secret\\secret.txt\" to /tmp/pov_ext_base_secret/secret.txt, a file OUTSIDE the configured external static folder /tmp/pov_ext_base, and returns a readable resource containing the secret marker. The PoV prints \"VULNERABLE\" and exits 0. After a correct patch (adding a path-separator boundary to the external-folder check), protectAgainstForExternal throws DirectoryTraversalDetection (or the handler returns null), and the PoV prints \"BLOCKED\" and exits 1.",
        "notes": "The PoV lives in package spark.resource so it can invoke the framework's protected getResource(String) that serves external static files, exercising the identical taint flow flagged by the finder (UriPath.canonical -> AbstractResourceHandler.addPaths -> ExternalResource/StringUtils.cleanPath -> DirectoryTraversal.protectAgainstForExternal -> getFile). run_pov.sh builds product classes (mvn -B -DskipTests package), resolves the dependency classpath, compiles the single PoV source, and runs it. The command must be executed inside /workspace/repo in the project container (JDK 8 / Maven). The exploit relies on backslash separators surviving canonicalization plus the boundary-less prefix comparison; it demonstrates reading an arbitrary file outside the intended static directory (sensitive-file disclosure).",
