        "patch_notes": "Added a private isWithin(resolvedPath, configuredFolder) helper to DirectoryTraversal that returns true only when resolvedPath equals configuredFolder or starts with configuredFolder + \"/\", enforcing a path-separator boundary. Both protectAgainstForExternal and protectAgainstInClassPath now delegate to it instead of calling startsWith() directly on the raw prefix, closing the sibling-prefix escape (e.g. base vs base_secret) while still normalizing backslashes to '/' before comparison. A null configured folder is treated as \"not within\" (rejected). Added DirectoryTraversalTest with two cases: a sibling directory sharing the configured folder's prefix is rejected (throws DirectoryTraversalDetection), and a file genuinely inside the configured folder is allowed. Verified: POV now exits 1 (BLOCKED) where it previously exited 0 (VULNERABLE); existing StaticFilesTest/StaticFilesTestExternal (10 tests) still pass.",
        "regression_commands": [
          "mvn -B -Dtest=DirectoryTraversalTest test",
          "mvn -B -Dtest=StaticFilesTest,StaticFilesTestExternal test"
        ],
        "risk_notes": "Low risk. The change only tightens the boundary check; legitimate requests for the configured folder and its descendants still resolve (confirmed by existing static-files tests). The same boundary is applied to the classpath check, which is the intended defensive symmetry. No dependency or API changes. Edge case: if the configured external/local folder is unset (null), requests are now rejected rather than throwing an NPE — a safe-by-default change.",
        "status": "patched",
        "summary": "Fixed CWE-022 path traversal in Spark's static-files boundary check. DirectoryTraversal used a raw startsWith() prefix comparison, so a sibling directory sharing the configured folder's name as a prefix (e.g. /tmp/pov_ext_base vs /tmp/pov_ext_base_secret) escaped the configured static root. Replaced the prefix check with a path-segment-boundary check (resolved path must equal the configured folder or begin with configuredFolder + \"/\") for both external and classpath resolution."
